{"componentChunkName":"component---src-templates-post-template-js","path":"/note-windows-dualboot-en","result":{"data":{"markdownRemark":{"id":"fdeec586-8452-58d4-acff-a22cbb90ba82","html":"<blockquote>\n<p>This page has been machine-translated from the <a href=\"/note-windows-dualboot\">original page</a>.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>In this article, I introduce how to recover when boot entries are corrupted in a Windows dual-boot environment and you can no longer boot an OS.</p>\n<p>Note that the “dual boot” in this article does not mean splitting the partitions of a single storage device and installing different OSs. Instead, it refers to connecting multiple storage devices to one machine and installing a different Windows OS on each one.</p>\n<!-- omit in toc -->\n<h2 id=\"table-of-contents\" style=\"position:relative;\"><a href=\"#table-of-contents\" aria-label=\"table of contents permalink\" class=\"anchor before\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\" height=\"16\" version=\"1.1\" viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" width=\"16\"><path fill-rule=\"evenodd\" d=\"M4 9h1v1H4c-1.5 0-3-1.69-3-3.5S2.55 3 4 3h4c1.45 0 3 1.69 3 3.5 0 1.41-.91 2.72-2 3.25V8.59c.58-.45 1-1.27 1-2.09C10 5.22 8.98 4 8 4H4c-.98 0-2 1.22-2 2.5S3 9 4 9zm9-3h-1v1h1c1 0 2 1.22 2 2.5S13.98 12 13 12H9c-.98 0-2-1.22-2-2.5 0-.83.42-1.64 1-2.09V6.25c-1.09.53-2 1.84-2 3.25C6 11.31 7.55 13 9 13h4c1.45 0 3-1.69 3-3.5S14.5 6 13 6z\"></path></svg></a>Table of Contents</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"#dual-boot-machine-setup\">Dual-Boot Machine Setup</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#how-to-restore-boot-entries\">How to Restore Boot Entries</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#summary\">Summary</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"dual-boot-machine-setup\" style=\"position:relative;\"><a href=\"#dual-boot-machine-setup\" aria-label=\"dual boot machine setup permalink\" class=\"anchor before\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\" height=\"16\" version=\"1.1\" viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" width=\"16\"><path fill-rule=\"evenodd\" d=\"M4 9h1v1H4c-1.5 0-3-1.69-3-3.5S2.55 3 4 3h4c1.45 0 3 1.69 3 3.5 0 1.41-.91 2.72-2 3.25V8.59c.58-.45 1-1.27 1-2.09C10 5.22 8.98 4 8 4H4c-.98 0-2 1.22-2 2.5S3 9 4 9zm9-3h-1v1h1c1 0 2 1.22 2 2.5S13.98 12 13 12H9c-.98 0-2-1.22-2-2.5 0-.83.42-1.64 1-2.09V6.25c-1.09.53-2 1.84-2 3.25C6 11.31 7.55 13 9 13h4c1.45 0 3-1.69 3-3.5S14.5 6 13 6z\"></path></svg></a>Dual-Boot Machine Setup</h2>\n<p>I have been using a Windows dual-boot setup on a ThinkPad E14 Gen6 that I purchased recently.</p>\n<p>Whether it is the Intel model or the AMD model, the ThinkPad E14 Gen6 supports two SSD slots and can be equipped with up to 64GB of DDR5-SODIMM RAM, making it a very customizable machine.</p>\n<p>The ThinkPad E14 Gen6 can hold two SSDs onboard: NVMe M.2 2280 and 2242.</p>\n<p>So right now, I have different Windows OS installations on the two SSDs installed in my ThinkPad E14 Gen6 and use them in a dual-boot setup.</p>\n<p>When you clean-install Windows on each disk in such an environment, a screen normally appears at startup that lets you choose which OS to boot, as shown below.</p>\n<p><span\n      class=\"gatsby-resp-image-wrapper\"\n      style=\"position: relative; display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; max-width: 636px; \"\n    >\n      <a\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-link\"\n    href=\"/static/3ef49a206d12a971760535a304db7520/9be90/image-20240928000730638.png\"\n    style=\"display: block\"\n    target=\"_blank\"\n    rel=\"noopener\"\n  >\n    <span\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-background-image\"\n    style=\"padding-bottom: 44.166666666666664%; position: relative; bottom: 0; left: 0; background-image: url('data:image/png;base64,'); background-size: cover; display: block;\"\n  ></span>\n  <picture>\n          <source\n              srcset=\"/static/3ef49a206d12a971760535a304db7520/8ac56/image-20240928000730638.webp 240w,\n/static/3ef49a206d12a971760535a304db7520/d3be9/image-20240928000730638.webp 480w,\n/static/3ef49a206d12a971760535a304db7520/6d494/image-20240928000730638.webp 636w\"\n              sizes=\"(max-width: 636px) 100vw, 636px\"\n              type=\"image/webp\"\n            />\n          <source\n            srcset=\"/static/3ef49a206d12a971760535a304db7520/8ff5a/image-20240928000730638.png 240w,\n/static/3ef49a206d12a971760535a304db7520/e85cb/image-20240928000730638.png 480w,\n/static/3ef49a206d12a971760535a304db7520/9be90/image-20240928000730638.png 636w\"\n            sizes=\"(max-width: 636px) 100vw, 636px\"\n            type=\"image/png\"\n          />\n          <img\n            class=\"gatsby-resp-image-image\"\n            src=\"/static/3ef49a206d12a971760535a304db7520/9be90/image-20240928000730638.png\"\n            alt=\"image-20240928000730638\"\n            title=\"image-20240928000730638\"\n            loading=\"lazy\"\n            style=\"width:100%;height:100%;margin:0;vertical-align:middle;position:absolute;top:0;left:0;\"\n          />\n        </picture>\n  </a>\n    </span></p>\n<p>Reference: <a href=\"https://weekly.ascii.jp/elem/000/001/546/1546225/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow noopener noreferrer\">Customize the default OS and display name in a Windows 10 multiboot setup - Weekly ASCII</a></p>\n<p>However, for example, if one of the OS installations is reset from the recovery options, the boot entry may be corrupted and you may no longer be able to boot that OS.</p>\n<p>Also, there are cases where you want to move an SSD that already has an OS installed and had been used in another machine into a different machine.</p>\n<p>In such situations, instead of clean-installing the OS again, you need to restore the dual-boot setup by restoring the boot entries.</p>\n<p>This time, I summarize how to restore boot entries by adding and editing them with the Windows BCDEdit tool.</p>\n<h2 id=\"how-to-restore-boot-entries\" style=\"position:relative;\"><a href=\"#how-to-restore-boot-entries\" aria-label=\"how to restore boot entries permalink\" class=\"anchor before\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\" height=\"16\" version=\"1.1\" viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" width=\"16\"><path fill-rule=\"evenodd\" d=\"M4 9h1v1H4c-1.5 0-3-1.69-3-3.5S2.55 3 4 3h4c1.45 0 3 1.69 3 3.5 0 1.41-.91 2.72-2 3.25V8.59c.58-.45 1-1.27 1-2.09C10 5.22 8.98 4 8 4H4c-.98 0-2 1.22-2 2.5S3 9 4 9zm9-3h-1v1h1c1 0 2 1.22 2 2.5S13.98 12 13 12H9c-.98 0-2-1.22-2-2.5 0-.83.42-1.64 1-2.09V6.25c-1.09.53-2 1.84-2 3.25C6 11.31 7.55 13 9 13h4c1.45 0 3-1.69 3-3.5S14.5 6 13 6z\"></path></svg></a>How to Restore Boot Entries</h2>\n<p>It is not mandatory, but if you have configured dual boot, I recommend running the <code class=\"language-text\">bcdedit</code> command in an administrator Command Prompt beforehand and saving the original boot entry configuration.</p>\n<p>If you save the original boot entry configuration, the editing work that follows will go more smoothly.</p>\n<p>After signing in to the Windows OS on the machine whose boot entry configuration you want to restore, first run the following command in an administrator Command Prompt.</p>\n<div class=\"gatsby-highlight\" data-language=\"bash\"><pre class=\"language-bash\"><code class=\"language-bash\">bcdedit /copy <span class=\"token punctuation\">{</span>current<span class=\"token punctuation\">}</span> /d <span class=\"token string\">\"OS1\"</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>This copies the current boot entry configuration and adds another one to the system.</p>\n<p>Here, you can use any name you like in place of <code class=\"language-text\">\"OS1\"</code>, as long as it lets you identify the boot entry.</p>\n<p>When you inspect the copied boot entry with the BCDEdit command, you can confirm that a new ID has been assigned to <code class=\"language-text\">identifier</code>.</p>\n<p><span\n      class=\"gatsby-resp-image-wrapper\"\n      style=\"position: relative; display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; max-width: 697px; \"\n    >\n      <a\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-link\"\n    href=\"/static/b7c6389f5137a7347a22a9264cd7a087/7422e/01.png\"\n    style=\"display: block\"\n    target=\"_blank\"\n    rel=\"noopener\"\n  >\n    <span\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-background-image\"\n    style=\"padding-bottom: 128.75%; position: relative; bottom: 0; left: 0; background-image: url('data:image/png;base64,'); background-size: cover; display: block;\"\n  ></span>\n  <picture>\n          <source\n              srcset=\"/static/b7c6389f5137a7347a22a9264cd7a087/8ac56/01.webp 240w,\n/static/b7c6389f5137a7347a22a9264cd7a087/d3be9/01.webp 480w,\n/static/b7c6389f5137a7347a22a9264cd7a087/458b7/01.webp 697w\"\n              sizes=\"(max-width: 697px) 100vw, 697px\"\n              type=\"image/webp\"\n            />\n          <source\n            srcset=\"/static/b7c6389f5137a7347a22a9264cd7a087/8ff5a/01.png 240w,\n/static/b7c6389f5137a7347a22a9264cd7a087/e85cb/01.png 480w,\n/static/b7c6389f5137a7347a22a9264cd7a087/7422e/01.png 697w\"\n            sizes=\"(max-width: 697px) 100vw, 697px\"\n            type=\"image/png\"\n          />\n          <img\n            class=\"gatsby-resp-image-image\"\n            src=\"/static/b7c6389f5137a7347a22a9264cd7a087/7422e/01.png\"\n            alt=\"img\"\n            title=\"img\"\n            loading=\"lazy\"\n            style=\"width:100%;height:100%;margin:0;vertical-align:middle;position:absolute;top:0;left:0;\"\n          />\n        </picture>\n  </a>\n    </span></p>\n<p>In the commands below, this <code class=\"language-text\">identifier</code> is used to modify the entry.</p>\n<p>First, use the following commands to specify the disk where the OS is installed for <code class=\"language-text\">device</code> and <code class=\"language-text\">osdevice</code>.</p>\n<p>The <code class=\"language-text\">\\Device\\HarddiskVolume6</code> part should be changed to match your environment.</p>\n<div class=\"gatsby-highlight\" data-language=\"bash\"><pre class=\"language-bash\"><code class=\"language-bash\">bcdedit /set <span class=\"token punctuation\">{</span><span class=\"token operator\">&lt;</span>identifier<span class=\"token operator\">></span><span class=\"token punctuation\">}</span> device <span class=\"token assign-left variable\">partition</span><span class=\"token operator\">=</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">\\</span>Device<span class=\"token punctuation\">\\</span>HarddiskVolume6\nbcdedit /set <span class=\"token punctuation\">{</span><span class=\"token operator\">&lt;</span>identifier<span class=\"token operator\">></span><span class=\"token punctuation\">}</span> osdevice <span class=\"token assign-left variable\">partition</span><span class=\"token operator\">=</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">\\</span>Device<span class=\"token punctuation\">\\</span>HarddiskVolume6</code></pre></div>\n<p>Next, use the following commands to set <code class=\"language-text\">recoverysequence</code> and <code class=\"language-text\">resumeobject</code>.</p>\n<p>If you saved the boot entry configuration in advance, you can reuse the required ID information here more smoothly.</p>\n<div class=\"gatsby-highlight\" data-language=\"bash\"><pre class=\"language-bash\"><code class=\"language-bash\">bcdedit /set <span class=\"token punctuation\">{</span><span class=\"token operator\">&lt;</span>identifier<span class=\"token operator\">></span><span class=\"token punctuation\">}</span> recoverysequence <span class=\"token punctuation\">{</span><span class=\"token operator\">&lt;</span>saved recoverysequence<span class=\"token operator\">></span><span class=\"token punctuation\">}</span>\nbcdedit /set <span class=\"token punctuation\">{</span><span class=\"token operator\">&lt;</span>identifier<span class=\"token operator\">></span><span class=\"token punctuation\">}</span> resumeobject <span class=\"token punctuation\">{</span><span class=\"token operator\">&lt;</span>saved resumeobject<span class=\"token operator\">></span><span class=\"token punctuation\">}</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>Finally, change the reconfigured entry to the default entry.</p>\n<div class=\"gatsby-highlight\" data-language=\"bash\"><pre class=\"language-bash\"><code class=\"language-bash\">bcdedit /default <span class=\"token punctuation\">{</span><span class=\"token operator\">&lt;</span>identifier<span class=\"token operator\">></span><span class=\"token punctuation\">}</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>When you set an entry as the default entry, it will be used as the automatically selected entry when the OS starts.</p>\n<p><span\n      class=\"gatsby-resp-image-wrapper\"\n      style=\"position: relative; display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; max-width: 703px; \"\n    >\n      <a\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-link\"\n    href=\"/static/08a07f08d59395cb8d61cfc865bdc058/242e2/02.png\"\n    style=\"display: block\"\n    target=\"_blank\"\n    rel=\"noopener\"\n  >\n    <span\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-background-image\"\n    style=\"padding-bottom: 62.916666666666664%; position: relative; bottom: 0; left: 0; background-image: url('data:image/png;base64,'); background-size: cover; display: block;\"\n  ></span>\n  <picture>\n          <source\n              srcset=\"/static/08a07f08d59395cb8d61cfc865bdc058/8ac56/02.webp 240w,\n/static/08a07f08d59395cb8d61cfc865bdc058/d3be9/02.webp 480w,\n/static/08a07f08d59395cb8d61cfc865bdc058/b1708/02.webp 703w\"\n              sizes=\"(max-width: 703px) 100vw, 703px\"\n              type=\"image/webp\"\n            />\n          <source\n            srcset=\"/static/08a07f08d59395cb8d61cfc865bdc058/8ff5a/02.png 240w,\n/static/08a07f08d59395cb8d61cfc865bdc058/e85cb/02.png 480w,\n/static/08a07f08d59395cb8d61cfc865bdc058/242e2/02.png 703w\"\n            sizes=\"(max-width: 703px) 100vw, 703px\"\n            type=\"image/png\"\n          />\n          <img\n            class=\"gatsby-resp-image-image\"\n            src=\"/static/08a07f08d59395cb8d61cfc865bdc058/242e2/02.png\"\n            alt=\"img\"\n            title=\"img\"\n            loading=\"lazy\"\n            style=\"width:100%;height:100%;margin:0;vertical-align:middle;position:absolute;top:0;left:0;\"\n          />\n        </picture>\n  </a>\n    </span></p>\n<p>If the boot entry configuration has been added correctly, restarting the OS will restore the dual-boot setup.</p>\n<h2 id=\"update-how-to-change-boot-entry-settings\" style=\"position:relative;\"><a href=\"#update-how-to-change-boot-entry-settings\" aria-label=\"update how to change boot entry settings permalink\" class=\"anchor before\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\" height=\"16\" version=\"1.1\" viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" width=\"16\"><path fill-rule=\"evenodd\" d=\"M4 9h1v1H4c-1.5 0-3-1.69-3-3.5S2.55 3 4 3h4c1.45 0 3 1.69 3 3.5 0 1.41-.91 2.72-2 3.25V8.59c.58-.45 1-1.27 1-2.09C10 5.22 8.98 4 8 4H4c-.98 0-2 1.22-2 2.5S3 9 4 9zm9-3h-1v1h1c1 0 2 1.22 2 2.5S13.98 12 13 12H9c-.98 0-2-1.22-2-2.5 0-.83.42-1.64 1-2.09V6.25c-1.09.53-2 1.84-2 3.25C6 11.31 7.55 13 9 13h4c1.45 0 3-1.69 3-3.5S14.5 6 13 6z\"></path></svg></a>Update: How to Change Boot Entry Settings</h2>\n<p>After configuring dual boot in this way, the default startup setting or display name you configured may sometimes be reset during Windows updates.</p>\n<p>In that case, you can restore the display name and automatic selection setting for a specific <code class=\"language-text\">identifier</code> and the corresponding entry with the following commands.</p>\n<div class=\"gatsby-highlight\" data-language=\"bash\"><pre class=\"language-bash\"><code class=\"language-bash\">bcdedit /set <span class=\"token punctuation\">{</span><span class=\"token operator\">&lt;</span>identifier<span class=\"token operator\">></span><span class=\"token punctuation\">}</span> description <span class=\"token operator\">&lt;</span>display name<span class=\"token operator\">></span>\nbcdedit /default <span class=\"token punctuation\">{</span><span class=\"token operator\">&lt;</span>identifier<span class=\"token operator\">></span><span class=\"token punctuation\">}</span></code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"summary\" style=\"position:relative;\"><a href=\"#summary\" aria-label=\"summary permalink\" class=\"anchor before\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\" height=\"16\" version=\"1.1\" viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" width=\"16\"><path fill-rule=\"evenodd\" d=\"M4 9h1v1H4c-1.5 0-3-1.69-3-3.5S2.55 3 4 3h4c1.45 0 3 1.69 3 3.5 0 1.41-.91 2.72-2 3.25V8.59c.58-.45 1-1.27 1-2.09C10 5.22 8.98 4 8 4H4c-.98 0-2 1.22-2 2.5S3 9 4 9zm9-3h-1v1h1c1 0 2 1.22 2 2.5S13.98 12 13 12H9c-.98 0-2-1.22-2-2.5 0-.83.42-1.64 1-2.09V6.25c-1.09.53-2 1.84-2 3.25C6 11.31 7.55 13 9 13h4c1.45 0 3-1.69 3-3.5S14.5 6 13 6z\"></path></svg></a>Summary</h2>\n<p>A note to my foolish past self, who panicked and recovered by clean-installing the OS after breaking a boot entry.</p>","fields":{"slug":"/note-windows-dualboot-en","tagSlugs":["/tag/notes/","/tag/windows/","/tag/english/"]},"frontmatter":{"date":"2024-09-27","description":"How to restore boot entries with BCDEdit after breaking them in a Windows dual-boot setup","tags":["Notes","Windows","English"],"title":"How to Restore Boot Entries with BCDEdit After Breaking Them in a Windows Dual-Boot Setup","socialImage":{"publicURL":"/static/0ed3dfee366f7eac0c4948175f42d312/note-windows-dualboot.png"}}}},"pageContext":{"slug":"/note-windows-dualboot-en"}},"staticQueryHashes":["251939775","401334301","825871152"]}